# Core Principles

Write code assuming another engineer will maintain it for years.

## TypeScript

- TypeScript strict mode is non-negotiable.
- Never use `any` unless absolutely unavoidable. Prefer `unknown` and narrow.
- Type every function signature (params + return). No implicit return types on exported functions.
- Use enums or `as const` objects for magic strings.

```ts
// ❌ BAD
const status = 'sent';
function handle(payload) { ... }

// ✅ GOOD
const STATUS = { idle: 'idle', sent: 'sent' } as const;
function handle(payload: SendOtpPayload): void { ... }
```

## Naming & exports

- Semantic naming for variables and functions. `isPhoneValid`, not `flag`.
- Prefer named exports over default exports.
- Centralize magic strings as constants (`src/constants/`).

## Style

- Use early returns to reduce nesting. Avoid deeply nested ternaries.
- Prefer explicitness over cleverness.
- Self-documenting code first; comments only when intent isn't obvious from code.
- Delete unused files, imports, and commented-out code on sight.
- Add comments only to explain WHY (intent, trade-off), never WHAT.

## Security & environment

- Use environment variables for configs (`EXPO_PUBLIC_*`).
- Never store secrets in code or in version control.
- Auth tokens go in secure storage (Keychain / Keystore via `expo-secure-store`),
  never in `AsyncStorage` or MMKV.
- Strip PII (phone, email, OTP, sessionId) from logs and analytics.

## Logging

- Use the centralized logging utility (`@/services/logger`).
- No `console.log` in committed code; lint blocks it on CI.
